Richard,

Thank you very much for the feedback. I greatly appreciate all the
work you do and you willingness to answer questions like mine. I will
take a look at the predict approach that you suggested.

To respond to your points:
2. I believe detectability does vary with julian date for a few
reasons - I detect prey species by sight and by sound. Sight is
affected by leaf our of deciduous trees and shrubs, sound in detected
by singing activity changing through the season. I know that the
assumption is that covariates can only change the scale of the
function and not the shape. I have validated this in Program Distance
by using only those detected by sight and only those by sound and I
still choose the same model shape.

3. The covariate by observation is whether the animal was detected by
sight or by sound. This has shown to be an important covariate.

Regarding BUGS, right now I have not replicated the distance algorithm
in a Bayesian model, but I am taking the abundance distributions from
Distance and placing that into BUGS along with my goshawk observations
and goshawk detection priors to complete my analysis. I have been
working on building the full BUGS model, but as you say, that is a
very intimidating task.

Thanks again.

-Rob

On Feb 21, 7:36 am, Richard Chandler <rchand...@usgs.gov> wrote:
> Hi Rob,
>
> (1) If you want to estimate density at a level greater than an individual
> transect, then you need to include "territory" as a covariate of density
> and then use predict(), not ranef(). This will give you estimates of
> density for each territory along with CIs. This will involve estimating
> many parameters, so you might want to try providing starting values.
>
> (2) You can include both julian date and observer as siteCovs in the
> analysis. However, I'm not sure how date could affect the distance at
> which you detect a bird.
>
> (3) What kind of observation-specific covariate could you have? These
> would need to be specific to each distance band.
>
> One last comment. To properly address all sources of variation in an
> analysis of the effects of prey density on goshawk occurrence, you would
> need to jointly analyze the goshawk data and the prey data. The "easiest"
> way to do this would be to use BUGS or JAGS, but this would be still be
> pretty hard, and so I think you are on the right track to first try to
> model goshawk occurrence using *estimates* of prey density.
>
> Best,
> Richard
