Subject: Modeling abundance as a function of obsCovs
Message-ID: <9QoulVoboLQ.9inaleA3DvUJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/9QoulVoboLQ/m/9inaleA3DvUJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

We are a new user of “unmarked”. We have repeated point count data for
45 sites. We are trying to model abundance as a function of habitat
covariates which change with time. The point count was done 28 times
and we have habitat data for each of those 28 occasions; the habitat
changes with each point count survey. There are two habitat
covariates; corn and disturbed habitat. We used them as observational
covariates as they change on each observation. We do not have any site
covariates in our model.
This is what we did to create an unmarked object
veg.umf<-unmarkedFramePCount(y=bald.eagle.y,
obsCovs=list(corn=habitat.corn,disturbed=habitat.disturbed))
The summary looks like this
> summary(veg.umf)
unmarkedFrame Object

45 sites
Maximum number of observations per site: 28
Mean number of observations per site: 28
Sites with at least one detection: 24

Tabulation of y observations:
   0    1    2    3   19 <NA>
1202   49    7    1    1    0

Observation-level covariates:
      corn            disturbed
 Min.   :0.000000   Min.   :0.000000
 1st Qu.:0.000000   1st Qu.:0.000000
 Median :0.000000   Median :0.000000
 Mean   :0.009722   Mean   :0.005556
 3rd Qu.:0.000000   3rd Qu.:0.000000
 Max.   :0.750000   Max.   :0.500000

When we model detection probability as a function of observation
covariates, it works fine
baldeagle.models[[1]]=pcount(~corn~1,veg.umf)

And the summary result looks like this

> summary(baldeagle.models[[1]])

Call:
pcount(formula = ~corn ~ 1, data = veg.umf)

Abundance (log-scale):
 Estimate    SE    z P(>|z|)
    0.888 0.248 3.57 0.00035

Detection (logit-scale):
            Estimate    SE      z  P(>|z|)
(Intercept)    -3.61 0.263 -13.69 1.21e-42
corn            1.97 0.920   2.14 3.26e-02

AIC: 677.05
Number of sites: 45
optim convergence code: 0
optim iterations: 32
Bootstrap iterations: 0

However, when we tried to model abundance as a function of  the same
observational covariates, it does not work

baldeagle.models[[1]]=pcount(~1~corn,veg.umf)

We got the error message
Error in eval(expr, envir, enclos) : object 'corn' not found.

How do we model abundance as a function of observational covariates?

